window.WUJIANG_STORIES = {
  "zh": {
    "01": "JA是我认识最久的孩子之一，是我第一次去球场的时候认识的。打完球他问我有没有水，能不能请他喝水。得到许可之后，又问我能不喝饮料，我同意后问我能不能喝奶茶。最后我点了两杯蜜雪冰城（我差点点了古茗，但是他调侃“你不会要请我喝古茗吧”），收买了这个“线人”。\n我认识的时候JA快读六年级，现在初一了。\n我来镇子上无非是找他钓钓鱼，打打篮球，偶尔打打台球。因为我球打得好，他一直找我，要我教他练球，后来他带着不少他的朋友一起找我练球，我也就慢慢成了个教练。\n\n给他相机是在我们第5，6次见面的时候，当时我快要回南京了，我们在面馆吃面。他一开始非常抗拒，怎么都不收。\n我问他为什么，他说：“有什么好拍的”。的确，他的朋友圈从来没有他拍的照片，都是游戏截图，视频转发之类。\n我问他他为什么不试一试，他好像有点不耐烦了都说道：“干嘛要拍照啊，麻烦死了。”\n“记录生活啊”\n“算了吧，我不拍了”\n我也不好强求他，只好作罢了。\n后来吃面的时候，我在他愣神的时候闪光灯拍了他一张，他咧着嘴从我手里抢走了相机，也报复性的闪了我一张。这是他用相机拍的第一张照片。\n至此，他好像也就认了，发现这也不是什么很可怕的任务。\n所有青年里，他是最快拍完的，第三天我问他进度的时候，他就说拍完了。我担心他在糊弄，但是他信誓旦旦地说拍的都是让他开心的事情，我也只能就怀揣着忐忑的心理去冲洗了。",
    "02": "我在钓鱼的时候碰到了CC和他的朋友，也就是胖哥。\nCC听说我从美国回来很感兴趣，我们也就加了好友。\nCC现在初三了，在镇子上读书。CC不打篮球，我们一般都是一起散散步，偶尔打打台球。\n他带了我去了镇子上的电影院，隐藏的台球厅和他们常去玩的地方。\n\n我给CC相机的时候，他倒是很爽快地接受了，就像给他拍肖像的时候一样。\n他拍的速度倒是比较慢，快两周才拍完第一卷。",
    "03": "贝利亚是球场上打球的一个孩子，五六年级的样子。\n在防守我的时候，他总喜欢喊各种奥特曼和怪兽的名字，而每次喊道贝利亚的时候我都投不进，后来他就叫我贝利亚了，我也学他，叫他贝利亚。\n贝利亚太小了，我还是犹豫是否应该给他相机，后来我碰巧见到了他的哥哥。\n他的哥哥高二了，在县里读书。\n我们是在球场上碰到的，作为“小孩场”里唯二的高中生，我们自然一直对位。他的篮下特别稳，基本没有空过。大家走了之后，我们又单挑了一会儿，边聊天，边单挑，便得知他是贝利亚的哥哥。\n\n他收到相机的时候也有些犹豫，不是那么果断的拒绝，而是或许不确定我的动机。\n跟他讲明白了只是一个个人的小项目之后，他也不再推辞。\n他后来跟我讲有些照片是贝利亚抢着拍的，他俩拍的也挺快的，没到一周就拍完了。",
    "04": "",
    "05": "鸡翅弟弟是JA的朋友，是在钓鱼的时候认识的，也上初一。\nJA带他跟我一起去江边钓鱼，他也对我很好奇，不像JA一直损我，他更“关照”我一点，帮着我说话。\n他的名字的由来有些典故。第三四次当我们一起打球的时候，我们被分在了对家。他一直主动过来防我，就算我们的身高差了起码20厘米。在我们大比分领先的时候，他偷偷从口袋里头拿了一包鸡翅，那种一元零食店里的，悄悄塞到了我的手里。\n那或许是我在乌江镇感到最感动的瞬间之一。他的手很小，两只手裹着那包鸡翅，攥紧了放在我的手里，然后又笑着让我别告诉别人。\n我回到酒店的时候，尝了尝那包鸡翅，或许是预期很低，抑或是其中的分量，我感觉它出奇的的香。\n\n后来我给鸡翅弟弟相机的时候，他还是笑嘻嘻的接受了，就像他面对什么都笑嘻嘻的一样。\n他的照片花了一周左右，还给我的时候也笑嘻嘻的。",
    "06": "大志是老板的微信名。\n味真面馆，这是我在乌江镇的第二个家。\n自从第一次在他们面馆吃了之后，我便认准了这家店，在镇子上所有的伙食，除了肯德基之外，全被他们家承包了，满打满算也快有二三十次了。\n每次进去见到他们两口子都格外亲切。\n\n很多次跟JA钓鱼前我们都去店里吃面，老板老板娘每次看着我们空着桶去空着桶回都会乐呵两句。\n老板娘还跟我们约了，说如果钓到了帮忙炖给我们吃，我们每次都说这次一定，但是到现在还没吃到炖鱼。\n\n老板很喜欢音乐，他的朋友圈有不少演唱会的视频。\n他知道我在搞乐队之后，每次我去店里他都问我乐队怎么样。\n虽说我们乐队翻唱的歌他大部分都不熟悉，窦唯他确实喜欢过。\n他跟我聊他年轻的时候如何喜欢窦唯，并且一直祝愿我们乐队要一直做下去。\n老板今年一月份左右开始戒烟，脸变得丑了不少，但还是会问我乐队的事情。",
    "07": "这是一些偶然碰见的，或者拍摄不多的肖像大全。"
  },
  "en": {
    "01": "JA is one of the kids I've known longest. We met the first time I went to the basketball court. After we finished playing, he asked if I had any water and whether I could get him some. Once I agreed, he asked if he could have a soft drink instead. When I said yes to that, he asked whether it could be milk tea. In the end I ordered two cups of Mixue (one of the most affordable milk tea brands)(I nearly went with Guming, a more average priced one, but he teased me: \"You're not seriously going to buy me Guming, are you?\"), and just like that, I had bought myself an \"informant.\"\nWhen I met him, JA was about to enter sixth grade. Now he's in seventh grade.\nMy trips to town were mostly about meeting up with him to fish, play basketball, and occasionally shoot pool. Because I was good at basketball, he kept asking me to coach him. Eventually he brought a bunch of his friends along to practice with me, and I gradually became a coach of sorts.\n\nI gave him the camera around our fifth or sixth meeting, when I was about to head back to Nanjing. We were having noodles at a noodle shop. At first he was dead set against it and wouldn't take it at all.\nI asked him why. \"What's there to photograph?\" he said. He had a point since his WeChat Moments had never featured a photo he'd taken, only game screenshots and reposted videos.\nI asked why he didn't at least give it a try. He was starting to sound impatient. \"Why bother taking photos? It's such a hassle.\"\n\"To document life,\" I said.\n\"Nah. I'm not doing it.\"\nI couldn't really force him, so I dropped it.\nLater, while we were eating, I caught him zoning out and snapped a photo of him with the flash (01). Grinning, he snatched the camera from my hands and retaliated by flashing me right back (02). It was the first photo he ever took with the camera.\nAfter that, he seemed to love it, as if he'd realized the task wasn't so scary after all.\nOf all the young people, he finished fastest. When I asked about his progress on the third day, he said he was already done. I worried he was just messing around, but he swore that everything he'd shot was something that made him happy. So, with a knot in my stomach, I took the film to be developed.",
    "02": "I met CC and his friend Pangge while I was fishing.\nCC was intrigued to hear that I'd come back from the States last summer, so we added each other on WeChat.\nCC is in ninth grade now and studies in town. He doesn't play basketball, so we usually just take walks together and occasionally shoot pool.\nHe took me to the town cinema, a hidden billiard hall, and their usual hangouts.\n\nWhen I gave CC the camera, he accepted it readily—just as he had when I took his portrait.\nHe was slower, though. It took him almost two weeks to finish the first roll.",
    "03": "Belial (A monster from Ultraman, 贝利亚) was a kid who played at the court, maybe in fifth or sixth grade.\nWhen he guarded me, he liked to shout the names of various Ultraman characters and monsters. Every time he shouted \"Belial,\" I missed my shot. Eventually he started calling me Belial, and I copied him and called him Belial too.\nBelial was too young, so I hesitated about whether to give him a camera. Then I happened to meet his older brother.\nHis brother was in eleventh grade and studied in the county high school.\nWe met at the court. As the only two high schoolers on the \"kids' court,\" we naturally ended up matched against each other. He was especially solid under the basket and hardly ever missed. After everyone left, we played one-on-one for a while longer. We talked as we played, and that was how I learned he was Belial's brother.\n\nWhen he received the camera, he also hesitated, perhaps uncertainty about my motives.\nOnce I explained that it was just a small personal project, he stopped resisting.\nLater he told me that Belial had grabbed the camera and taken some of the photos himself. The two of them finished quickly—in under a week.",
    "04": "",
    "05": "Chicken-Wing Bro is JA's friend. I met him while fishing, and he's also in seventh grade.\nJA brought him along to fish with me by the river. He was curious about me too. Unlike JA, who was always teasing me, he looked out for me a little more and spoke up for me.\nThere's a story behind his name. The third or fourth time we played basketball together, we ended up on opposing teams. He kept coming over to guard me, even though I was at least 20 centimeters taller. When we were well ahead, he secretly pulled a pack of chicken wings out of his pocket, the kind from dollar snack shops, and slipped it into my hand.\nThat may have been one of the most touching moments I had in Wujiang. His hands were small; he cupped the packet of chicken wings in both of them, pressed it firmly into my palm, then smiled and told me not to tell anyone.\nWhen I got back to the hotel, I tried the chicken wings. Maybe because my expectations were low, or maybe because of the weight of the gesture, they tasted surprisingly good.\n\nLater, when I gave Chicken-Wing Bro the camera, he accepted it with the same grin he wore for everything.\nHis photos took him about a week, and he was still grinning when he gave the camera back.",
    "06": "Dazhi is the boss's WeChat name.\nWeizhen Noodle Shop was my second home in Wujiang.\nAfter my first meal there, I was set on this place. Apart from KFC, every meal I ate in town was from their shop—by my rough count, twenty or thirty times.\nEvery time I walked in, seeing the two of them felt especially warm.\n\nMany times, before going fishing with JA, we'd stop at the shop for noodles. The owner and his wife would always laugh when they saw us head out with empty buckets and come back with empty buckets.\nThe owner's wife even promised that if we caught anything, she'd stew it for us. Every time we'd say, \"This time for sure,\" but we still haven't had that stewed fish.\n\nThe owner loved music, and his WeChat Moments were full of concert videos.\nAfter he learned I was in a band, he asked me how the band was doing every time I came in.\nMost of the songs our band covered were unfamiliar to him, but he really did like Dou Wei back in the day.\nHe told me about how much he'd loved Dou Wei when he was young, and he always wished for our band to keep going.\nThe owner quit smoking around January this year. He's gotten noticeably uglier since then, but he still asks about the band.",
    "07": "This is a collection of portraits of people I met by chance or photographed only a handful of times."
  }
};
